{"gid":"2026_02_IND_KC","week":2,"away":"IND","home":"KC","away_col":"#4F9BE8","home_col":"#FF4D57","spread":5.5,"total":47.5,"fav":"KC","hpts":26,"apts":21,"kickoff":"Sun Sep 20 \u00b7 20:20","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 IND concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><p>The <strong>KC</strong> host the <strong>IND</strong> as favorites of <strong>5.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>47.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>KC 26\u201321</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>40% of its snaps with KC in front</strong>, <strong>36% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with KC chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to KC</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while IND leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p><strong>Patrick Mahomes</strong> adds rushing value \u2014 he scrambles on 4.8% of dropbacks, live for rush-yard and anytime-TD props.</p><p>Tough sledding for <strong>Jonathan Taylor</strong> \u2014 KC are stingy against the run (108 yds/game, well below the ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Patrick Mahomes</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 IND allow <strong>253 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Travis Kelce</strong> has room to work \u2014 IND allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Alec Pierce</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 KC hold WR1s to just 50 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p>In the red zone KC throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Rashee Rice</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to KC \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>KC \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>KC stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>50%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Kenneth Walker III</strong> (23 carries, 101 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though KC's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Rashee Rice</strong> projects for <strong>12 targets and 101 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Travis Kelce</strong> (TE, 8 targets, 65 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>IND \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>IND are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 IND project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Jonathan Taylor</strong>'s rushing projection (19 carries, 93 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Alec Pierce</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, IND funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 75 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Tyler Warren</strong> (TE, 10 targets, 68 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"IND","col":"#4F9BE8","players":[{"name":"Daniel Jones","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":190.23,"book":null,"over_th":176.88,"under_th":197.49,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":0.98,"book":null,"over_th":0.93,"under_th":1.12,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":14.5,"fair":"6/1","book":null,"value":null},"d":{"pass_yards":{"